<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-panel ifm-takeaways"} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-panel ifm-takeaways"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Key points</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"className":"ifm-ticks"} -->
<ul class="wp-block-list ifm-ticks"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>A small business website has one job: turn a visitor into an enquiry, a booking or a sale.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>The first screen must say what you do, where, and how to get in touch.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Five pages are enough for most small businesses.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Speed and a working enquiry form matter more than clever design.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most visitors to a small business website arrive on a phone, look at the first screen for a few seconds and decide whether to stay. A good site makes that decision easy. This guide goes through what to include, page by page, and what you can safely leave out.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The first screen</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Before anyone scrolls, they should be able to answer three questions: what do you do, where do you do it, and how do I contact you? That means a plain headline ("Garden design in Bristol" is better than "Welcome to our website"), one sentence of detail and one clear button, such as "Get a free quote" or "Call now".</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The five pages most businesses need</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Home:</strong> who you are, what you offer and the main action, with short routes to everything else.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Services:</strong> what you do, described the way a customer would search for it. Give each main service its own section or page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Prices or a price guide:</strong> even a "from" figure saves both sides time and builds trust.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>About:</strong> who is behind the business, where you are based and why someone should choose you. Real photos help.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Contact:</strong> a form, your phone number, your email, your opening hours and the area you cover.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A sole trader can start with fewer. A business with several services or locations will need more, usually one page for each service and each area served.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Things every page should have</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Your phone number or main button visible without scrolling.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Text large enough to read on a phone without zooming.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Photos of your real work, premises or products.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>A clear page title that says what the page is about, because that is what shows in Google.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>A link back to the home page and to the contact page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

