<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">An enquiry form that works</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A surprising number of small business forms send messages to an old address or straight to spam. Keep the form short: name, contact details and message. Test it yourself after launch and again every few months. Add a note saying how quickly you reply, and then reply that quickly.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Speed</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A slow page loses people before they read a word. The usual cause is oversized images. Photos straight from a camera can be twenty times larger than they need to be. Compress them, use modern image formats and switch on caching. You can test any page free with Google's PageSpeed Insights.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The legal basics for a UK business website</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>UK businesses must show certain details on their website. A sole trader should show their own name and a business address. A limited company must show its registered name, company number and registered office. You also need a privacy policy if you collect personal details through a form, and you must ask for consent before using non-essential cookies such as analytics. If you sell online, terms and a refund policy are expected.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What you can leave out</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sliders, autoplay video, pop-ups, music, a news section you will never update and long "mission statements" all slow a site down or get in the way. If something does not help a visitor decide or get in touch, leave it out.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How many pages does a small business website need?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most need about five: home, services, prices, about and contact. Add a page for each main service or area once the basics are in place.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Should I build it myself?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You can, with a website builder, if you have the time. The usual weak spots on self-built sites are speed, wording and the enquiry form, so test those carefully.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Do I need a blog?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Only if you will keep it up. A few useful guides that answer customers' questions are worth more than a news page last updated two years ago.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Which platform should I use?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>WordPress suits most small businesses because you own it and any developer can work on it. A hosted builder is simpler if you want no technical upkeep at all.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

